# Java and Sling Models (core)

- Compile target is `release` 11. No records, sealed types or pattern-matching `switch`.
- Public model interface in `com.airline.travel.core.models`, with the implementation in `models.impl`. Annotate the implementation with `@Model(adaptables = SlingHttpServletRequest.class, adapters = {Interface.class, ComponentExporter.class}, resourceType = ..., defaultInjectionStrategy = OPTIONAL)`.
- Extending a Core Component: delegate with `@Self @Via(type = ResourceSuperType.class)` and wrap only what changes (see `TeaserImpl`, `RelSafeLink`). Don't copy Core HTL or code.
- HTL gets ready-to-render values. Selection, sorting, sanitising and ID generation belong in Java.
- IDs: `ComponentUtils.generateId(prefix, path)` so they are stable across renders and match the data layer.
- Heading levels come from the template policy, validated to `h2`–`h6`, with a safe default.
- OSGi services: `@Component` + `@Designate` with a `@ObjectClassDefinition`. Put limits in config (see `TravelQuestionServiceImpl`: allowed root, max items, max fragments scanned) and ship defaults in `ui.config`.
- Repository paths from authors: normalise them and check they are under an allowed root. Reject `..` and sibling prefixes.
- Rich text from fragments goes through `RichTextSanitizer` before it is rendered or put into JSON-LD.
- No `ResourceResolverFactory.getAdministrativeResourceResolver`. Use the request resolver, or a service user defined by repoinit.
- Logging: SLF4J, parameterised messages, `debug` for expected empty states. Never log PII.
- Every model or service change needs AEM Mocks tests. Coverage below 80 % fails the build.
